  <!-- ===================== RESPONSE ===================== -->
  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">07</span><h2>Recommended Response</h2></div>
    <p>Containment, eradication, and recovery guidance a SOC would attach to this incident.</p>
    <div class="eyebrow" style="margin:22px 0 6px;color:var(--crit);">Immediate · Containment</div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Isolate <code class="inl">winserv2025</code> (MDE did this automatically — confirm it held).</li>
      <li>Treat <code class="inl">mrdaniel98</code> and <strong>every credential cached on the host</strong> as compromised.</li>
      <li>Preserve <code class="inl">dumpert.dmp</code> for forensic analysis before removal.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="eyebrow" style="margin:22px 0 6px;color:var(--amber);">Eradication</div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Remove the payload and staging directory.</li>
      <li>Force password resets for the account and any identity that logged on to the host; rotate cached local-admin and service-account secrets.</li>
      <li>Invalidate active Kerberos tickets; consider <code class="inl">krbtgt</code> reset if domain-joined and broader compromise is suspected.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="eyebrow" style="margin:22px 0 6px;color:var(--teal);">Recovery &amp; Hardening</div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Enable <strong>LSA Protection (RunAsPPL)</strong> and <strong>Credential Guard</strong> to make LSASS dumping materially harder.</li>
      <li>Deploy the Section 6 analytics as scheduled detections; add custom indicators for the payload hashes.</li>
      <li>Restrict PowerShell with Constrained Language Mode and script-block logging where feasible.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== ANALYST NOTES ===================== -->
  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">08</span><h2>Analyst Notes</h2></div>
    <ul class="clean">
      <li><strong>Confirming impact is the analyst's job, not an assumption.</strong> The dump file size was the difference between "a tool ran" and "credentials were stolen."</li>
      <li><strong>Verify before dismissing, and dismiss when verified.</strong> The Edge <code class="inl">Local State</code> rename was worth one query to clear; chasing it further would have wasted time on the real lead.</li>
      <li><strong>Reputation scores are an input, not a verdict.</strong> Both the malicious binary (52/70) and the benign domain (0/91) were read in context.</li>
      <li><strong>Tooling can interfere with itself.</strong> MDE Attack Disruption auto-isolated the host mid-emulation; recognising that the EDR was severing the process chain, and managing around it safely, is itself a real operational skill.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

